UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 3: Digital basics and data', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Computing systems follow input → process → output, often with storage. Input devices collect data; processing transforms it; output presents results; storage keeps data for later.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nLabel input/process/output for: pressing a calculator keys for 12 × 4 and seeing 48.\n\nFEEDBACK\nInput: keys 12, ×, 4. Process: multiply. Output: 48 on screen.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCalling the screen an input because you look at it. Looking is using output; typing/tapping is input.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDescribe a school-safe device using input, process, output, and whether anything is stored. Do not describe hacking.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Information and Software Technology","learning_intention":"Computing systems follow input → process → output, often with storage. Input devices collect data; processing transforms it; output presents results; storage keeps data for later.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["IST5-DAT-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Describe a school-safe device using input, process, output, and whether anything is stored. Do not describe hacking. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using input on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Calling the screen an input because you look at it.. Correction: Input: keys 12, ×, 4. Process: multiply. Output: 48 on screen.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Calling the screen an input because you look at it. Looking is using output; typing/tapping is input. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating input on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with input, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about input with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for input. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for input. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Input: keys 12, ×, 4. Process: multiply. Output: 48 on screen","Calling the screen an input because you look at it","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result","Copy the model objects and call that independent work"],"question":"Label input/process/output for: pressing a calculator keys for 12 × 4 and seeing 48. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for input; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of process with clear evidence","Calling the screen an input because you look at it","Computing systems follow input → process → output, often with storage","Input: keys 12, ×, 4. Process: multiply. Output: 48 on screen"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with input?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named process, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply input to new material and name the process used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Calling the screen an input because you look at it","Name process only, without linking it to input"],"question":"Which response best applies input with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on input. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing","Calling the screen an input because you look at it","Input: keys 12, ×, 4. Process: multiply. Output: 48 on screen","Start making with no criteria or safety check"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing input?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling the screen an input because you look at it","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves input","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about input?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Computing systems follow input → process → output, often with storage.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling the screen an input because you look at it","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","Typing a search (input) → software finds matches (process) → results list (output), with history optionally stored"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about input?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for input?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling the screen an input because you look at it","Name process only, with no linked example","Describe a school-safe device using input, process, output, and whether anything is stored","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies input to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about input with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating input on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using input on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for input. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with input, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Calling the screen an input because you look at it.. Correction: Input: keys 12, ×, 4. Process: multiply. Output: 48 on screen.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-information-software-technology-y9-w02-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 IST alignment. Official outcome codes must match the published IST syllabus page; catalogue codes preferred when present. Verification pending. Not a hacking guide. Follow school acceptable-use rules.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise input.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Information and Software Technology","success_criteria":["I can use input with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify process and explain why it matters.","I can practise output independently on a new prompt.","I can notice store needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Computing systems follow input → process → output, often with storage.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Computing systems follow input → process → output, often with storage"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about input?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTyping a search (input) → software finds matches (process) → results list (output), with history optionally stored. A thermometer app: sensor input → convert to °C → display number.\n\nTRY FIRST\nLabel input/process/output for: pressing a calculator keys for 12 × 4 and seeing 48.\n\nFEEDBACK\nInput: keys 12, ×, 4. Process: multiply. Output: 48 on screen.","explain":"Computing systems follow input → process → output, often with storage. Input devices collect data; processing transforms it; output presents results; storage keeps data for later.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Calling the screen an input because you look at it. Looking is using output; typing/tapping is input. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Calculator keys in, result out.","caption":"Input → process → output.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Input</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">keys</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Output</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">48</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Typing a search (input) → software finds matches (process) → results list (output), with history optionally stored. A th Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Typing a search (input) → software finds matches (process) → results list (output), with history optionally stored. A th Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about input?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTyping a search (input) → software finds matches (process) → results list (output), with history optionally stored. A thermometer app: sensor input → convert to °C → display number. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Processing transforms inputs to outputs.","caption":"No hacking content.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Process</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\